---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const steps = [
  {
    n: "0",
    title: "Always-on hardware (level 0)",
    body:
      "Some silicon is present and powered the whole time rather than being a step in the sequence: the CPU and its caches execute whatever the current stage points them at, immutable Trusted ROM holds the very first code, and on-chip Trusted SRAM is where the writable stages (BL1's data, then BL2, then BL31 and BL32) are copied and run. That is why they carry no step number and are tagged as level 0.",
  },
  {
    n: "1",
    title: "Reset",
    body:
      "The primary CPU is released from reset. Its registers are reset to known values and the program counter is set to the reset vector, a fixed address that points into Trusted ROM. On a multicore SoC the other cores are held in a safe state until the primary has done enough setup to bring them up (later handled by PSCI in BL31).",
  },
  {
    n: "2",
    title: "BL1, Application Processor Trusted ROM (EL3)",
    body:
      "The application processor is the main CPU cluster that will eventually run the OS (as opposed to a separate System Control Processor that some SoCs use for power and clocks). Immutable code in its Trusted ROM runs first at EL3. It installs exception vectors and runs the CPU reset handler, then brings up the platform: raise the clock/PLL to full speed, enable the Trusted Watchdog (which resets the SoC if boot ever hangs in a loop), set up the console, enable interconnect coherency and the MMU, and configure the flash it loads from. It reads a root-key hash from OTP/eFuses, uses the crypto engine to verify the BL2 image, copies it into Trusted SRAM and hands control to BL2 at Secure-EL1.",
  },
  {
    n: "3",
    title: "BL2, Trusted Boot Firmware (Secure-EL1)",
    body:
      "BL2 runs from Trusted SRAM and does the platform security setup, including programming the TrustZone controller (TZC) that splits DRAM and peripherals into secure and non-secure regions. It then loads the later stages (the BL3x images) from the Firmware Image Package (FIP) in flash, verifying each against the same root of trust: the EL3 runtime (BL31), the optional secure payload (BL32) and the non-trusted firmware (BL33). BL2 raises an SMC so BL1 turns off the MMU, flushes caches and jumps to BL31 at EL3.",
  },
  {
    n: "4",
    title: "BL31, EL3 Runtime Software (EL3)",
    body:
      "BL31 is the resident EL3 runtime; it never exits. It re-initialises the architecture, sets up the generic interrupt controller (GIC), initialises PSCI power management (secondary-CPU boot, hotplug, idle) and registers the runtime services that the normal world calls into later through the SMC (Secure Monitor Call) instruction. It stays in Trusted SRAM for the life of the system.",
  },
  {
    n: "5",
    title: "BL32, Secure-EL1 Payload (Secure-EL1, optional)",
    body:
      "If present, BL32 is the Trusted OS (or other secure payload) running in the secure world at Secure-EL1. BL31 initialises it through a Secure-EL1 Payload Dispatcher (SPD) and routes secure/normal world switches to it over SMCs. It is optional; on AArch32 there is no BL31, and BL32 (for example SP_MIN) plays the EL3 runtime role instead.",
  },
  {
    n: "6",
    title: "BL33, Non-trusted Firmware (EL2/EL1)",
    body:
      "BL31 sets up the normal-world context and jumps to BL33 at the highest non-secure exception level (EL2 if available, otherwise EL1). BL33 is normal-world firmware such as UEFI or U-Boot, running from non-secure DRAM. It is the first non-secure code to run, and it goes on to boot the operating system.",
  },
];

const connections = [
  {
    title: "Debug port (JTAG/SWD)",
    body:
      "wires into the CPU's own debug access port, independent of the boot code. On a production secure part it is disabled by a fuse or gated behind an authenticated debug challenge, because otherwise it would bypass the entire chain of trust.",
  },
  {
    title: "Boot straps and the reset vector",
    body:
      "a handful of pins latched once at reset (together with the PROGRAMMABLE_RESET_ADDRESS option) decide where the primary CPU starts and which image BL1 loads. It is a one-time hardware input, not something software chooses.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Hardware">
  <p class="mono-eyebrow mb-1"><span class="text-accent-blue">$</span> dmesg | grep -i boot</p>
  <h1 class="glitch is-intro mb-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-brand-text" data-text="Hardware">Hardware</h1>

  <!-- What is it? -->
  <div class="mb-10 rounded-lg border-l-2 border-accent-green/40 bg-accent-green/5 px-4 py-3">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-sm font-bold text-brand-text">What is it?</h2>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-brand-body">
      A cheat sheet on what an Arm SoC does between power-on and the operating system. First topic: the
      Arm Trusted Firmware-A (TF-A) secure boot chain, BL1 &rarr; BL2 &rarr; BL31 / BL32 &rarr; BL33, the exception
      levels and memory regions each stage uses, and where a verification failure stops everything.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ====== SECURE BOOT ====== -->
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="mono-eyebrow mb-2">// power-on &rarr; chain of trust</p>
    <h2 class="glitch mb-4 text-xl font-semibold text-brand-text" data-text="Anatomy of a secure boot">Anatomy of a secure boot</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 text-brand-body">
      A walkthrough of a secure boot on an Arm application processor, following the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/ARM-software/arm-trusted-firmware/blob/master/docs/design/firmware-design.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:text-accent-emerald">Arm Trusted Firmware-A (TF-A)</a>{" "}
      cold boot: the chain of trust from the reset vector in Trusted ROM, through BL1 and BL2, into the EL3 runtime
      (BL31), the optional secure payload (BL32) and the non-trusted firmware (BL33) that finally boots the OS in DRAM.
      Here <strong class="text-brand-text">BL means Boot Loader stage</strong>, so BL1 is stage 1, BL2 is stage 2, and
      BL31, BL32 and BL33 are the three parts of stage 3. The number in the name already tells you the stage, which is
      why the boxes below only carry the memory and exception-level detail.
    </p>

    <!-- ===== Unified diagram: die photo + full boot anatomy ===== -->
    <figure class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-white p-4 sm:p-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 1040" class="soc-svg mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-[980px] min-w-[900px]" role="img" aria-labelledby="soc-uni-title soc-uni-desc">
        <title id="soc-uni-title">Arm TF-A secure boot, mapped onto a real Broadcom BCM7019 die</title>
        <desc id="soc-uni-desc">A single diagram combining a real die photograph of the Broadcom BCM7019 Arm SoC (left) with the full Arm Trusted Firmware-A cold boot anatomy. Labelled callouts for the CPU cluster, clock/PLL, watchdog, boot ROM, OTP/eFuses, crypto engine and on-chip Trusted SRAM point onto the die and each carries a coloured arrow to the boot stage it serves in the Reset, BL1, BL2, BL31, BL32, BL33 spine on the right. Flash and DRAM are shown as off-die board chips. A verification-failure branch halts the SoC, a dashed line marks the secure/normal world boundary, and the operating system starts once BL33 runs. Block positions on the photo are approximate because the die is not officially annotated.</desc>
        <defs>
          <marker id="abig" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M1.5 1L8.5 5L1.5 9" fill="none" stroke="context-stroke" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- top labels -->
        <text class="divider-label" x="24" y="40">Physical chip (Broadcom BCM7019 die, block positions approximate)</text>
        <text class="divider-label" x="858" y="40">Boot execution chain</text>

        <!-- ===== Die photo ===== -->
        <image href="/images/broadcom-bcm7019-die.jpg" x="24" y="74" width="520" height="446" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/>
        <rect x="24" y="74" width="520" height="446" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="#cbd5e1" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text class="divider-label" x="24" y="540">Real die shot (public domain). Coloured dots are illustrative block positions.</text>

        <!-- ===== Leaders from on-die parts to the die ===== -->
        <g stroke-linecap="round">
          <line x1="580" y1="101" x2="310" y2="297" stroke="#5f5e5a" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
          <line x1="580" y1="171" x2="138" y2="132" stroke="#5f5e5a" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
          <line x1="580" y1="241" x2="201" y2="114" stroke="#5f5e5a" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
          <line x1="580" y1="311" x2="86"  y2="252" stroke="#0f6e56" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
          <line x1="580" y1="381" x2="55"  y2="136" stroke="#993556" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
          <line x1="580" y1="451" x2="268" y2="194" stroke="#993c1d" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
          <line x1="580" y1="521" x2="461" y2="359" stroke="#0f6e56" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
        </g>
        <g stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.4">
          <circle cx="310" cy="297" r="3.6" fill="#5f5e5a"/>
          <circle cx="138" cy="132" r="3.6" fill="#5f5e5a"/>
          <circle cx="201" cy="114" r="3.6" fill="#5f5e5a"/>
          <circle cx="86"  cy="252" r="3.6" fill="#0f6e56"/>
          <circle cx="55"  cy="136" r="3.6" fill="#993556"/>
          <circle cx="268" cy="194" r="3.6" fill="#993c1d"/>
          <circle cx="461" cy="359" r="3.6" fill="#0f6e56"/>
        </g>

        <!-- ===== Boot spine (grouped, white stage boxes) ===== -->
        <g class="c-stage">
          <rect x="858" y="74" width="132" height="54" rx="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <text class="th" x="924" y="96" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Reset</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="113" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">power-on</text>
        </g>
        <g class="c-purple badge"><circle cx="978" cy="88" r="9"/><text class="ts" x="978" y="88" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">1</text></g>
        <line x1="924" y1="128" x2="924" y2="144" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <g class="c-stage">
          <rect x="858" y="144" width="132" height="264" rx="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <text class="th" x="924" y="172" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">BL1</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="190" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Trusted ROM, EL3</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="212" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Brings up the platform,</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="228" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">verifies and loads BL2.</text>
        </g>
        <g class="c-purple badge"><circle cx="978" cy="158" r="9"/><text class="ts" x="978" y="158" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">2</text></g>
        <line x1="924" y1="408" x2="924" y2="424" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <g class="c-stage">
          <rect x="858" y="424" width="132" height="194" rx="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <text class="th" x="924" y="452" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">BL2</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="470" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">SRAM, Secure-EL1</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="492" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Locks TrustZone,</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="508" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">loads the BL3x images.</text>
        </g>
        <g class="c-purple badge"><circle cx="978" cy="438" r="9"/><text class="ts" x="978" y="438" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">3</text></g>
        <line x1="924" y1="618" x2="924" y2="634" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <g class="c-stage">
          <rect x="858" y="634" width="132" height="64" rx="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <text class="th" x="924" y="660" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">BL31</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="678" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">EL3 runtime</text>
        </g>
        <g class="c-purple badge"><circle cx="978" cy="648" r="9"/><text class="ts" x="978" y="648" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">4</text></g>
        <line x1="924" y1="698" x2="924" y2="714" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <g class="c-stage">
          <rect x="858" y="714" width="132" height="64" rx="10" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/>
          <text class="th" x="924" y="740" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">BL32</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="758" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">optional</text>
        </g>
        <g class="c-purple badge"><circle cx="978" cy="728" r="9"/><text class="ts" x="978" y="728" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">5</text></g>
        <line x1="924" y1="778" x2="924" y2="800" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <g class="c-stage">
          <rect x="858" y="800" width="132" height="54" rx="10" stroke-width="1.4"/>
          <text class="th" x="924" y="820" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">BL33</text>
          <text class="ts" x="924" y="837" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">DRAM, EL2/EL1</text>
        </g>
        <g class="c-purple badge"><circle cx="978" cy="814" r="9"/><text class="ts" x="978" y="814" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">6</text></g>

        <!-- ===== Hardware / memory cards (name + role) ===== -->
        <!-- c1 CPU cluster -> Reset -->
        <g class="c-gray"><rect x="580" y="74" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="95" dominant-baseline="central">CPU cluster</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="112" dominant-baseline="central">Arm cores; runs every stage.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="101" x2="856" y2="101" stroke="#5f5e5a" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c2 Clock / PLL -> BL1 -->
        <g class="c-gray"><rect x="580" y="144" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="165" dominant-baseline="central">Clock / PLL</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="182" dominant-baseline="central">Ramps the CPU to full speed.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="171" x2="856" y2="171" stroke="#5f5e5a" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c3 Watchdog -> BL1 -->
        <g class="c-gray"><rect x="580" y="214" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="235" dominant-baseline="central">Watchdog</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="252" dominant-baseline="central">Resets the SoC if boot hangs.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="241" x2="856" y2="241" stroke="#5f5e5a" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c4 Boot ROM -> BL1 -->
        <g class="c-teal"><rect x="580" y="284" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="305" dominant-baseline="central">Boot ROM</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="322" dominant-baseline="central">Immutable first code; root of trust.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="311" x2="856" y2="311" stroke="#0f6e56" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c5 OTP / eFuses -> BL1 -->
        <g class="c-pink"><rect x="580" y="354" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="375" dominant-baseline="central">OTP / eFuses</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="392" dominant-baseline="central">Burned-in root-key hash + config.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="381" x2="856" y2="381" stroke="#993556" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c6 Crypto engine -> BL2 -->
        <g class="c-coral"><rect x="580" y="424" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="445" dominant-baseline="central">Crypto engine</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="462" dominant-baseline="central">Verifies each image signature.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="451" x2="856" y2="451" stroke="#993c1d" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c7 Trusted SRAM -> BL2 -->
        <g class="c-teal"><rect x="580" y="494" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="515" dominant-baseline="central">Trusted SRAM</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="532" dominant-baseline="central">On-chip RAM: BL2/BL31/BL32 run here.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="521" x2="856" y2="521" stroke="#0f6e56" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c8 Flash (off-die) -> BL2 -->
        <g class="c-amber"><rect x="580" y="564" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="585" dominant-baseline="central">Flash storage (off-die)</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="602" dominant-baseline="central">Board chip; holds the FIP images.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="591" x2="856" y2="591" stroke="#854f0b" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- c9 DRAM (off-die) -> BL33 -->
        <g class="c-blue"><rect x="580" y="800" width="250" height="54" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="594" y="821" dominant-baseline="central">DRAM (off-die)</text>
          <text class="ts" x="594" y="838" dominant-baseline="central">Board memory; BL33 and OS run here.</text></g>
        <line x1="832" y1="827" x2="856" y2="827" stroke="#185fa5" stroke-width="2.4" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- ===== Verification-failure branch ===== -->
        <g class="c-red"><rect x="598" y="650" width="214" height="60" rx="10" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="705" y="672" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Verification failed</text>
          <text class="ts" x="705" y="690" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Any BL check fails: halt or recover.</text></g>
        <path d="M858 392 L838 392 L838 668 L814 674" class="wire-fail" fill="none" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>
        <path d="M858 590 L846 590 L846 694 L814 690" class="wire-fail" fill="none" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>

        <!-- ===== Secure / normal boundary ===== -->
        <line x1="16" y1="779" x2="992" y2="779" class="divider"/>
        <text class="divider-label" x="844" y="772" text-anchor="end">&#8593; Secure world (EL3 / Secure-EL1)</text>
        <text class="divider-label" x="844" y="793" text-anchor="end">&#8595; Normal world (EL2 / EL1)</text>

        <!-- ===== OS world ===== -->
        <path d="M924 854 L924 878 L147 878 L147 933" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.4" fill="none" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>
        <rect class="os-world" x="16" y="900" width="976" height="132" rx="14"/>
        <text class="divider-label" x="32" y="922">Operating system, normal world (booted by BL33)</text>
        <g class="c-green"><rect x="40" y="945" width="215" height="66" rx="10" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="147" y="972" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">OS kernel</text>
          <text class="ts" x="147" y="992" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">takes control</text></g>
        <g class="c-amber"><rect x="280" y="945" width="215" height="66" rx="10" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="387" y="972" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Mount FS</text>
          <text class="ts" x="387" y="992" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">files appear</text></g>
        <g class="c-gray"><rect x="520" y="945" width="215" height="66" rx="10" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="627" y="972" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">init / systemd</text>
          <text class="ts" x="627" y="992" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">starts services</text></g>
        <g class="c-gray"><rect x="760" y="945" width="215" height="66" rx="10" stroke-width="1"/>
          <text class="th" x="867" y="972" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">Shell / scripts</text>
          <text class="ts" x="867" y="992" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">bash, python&#8230;</text></g>
        <line x1="255" y1="978" x2="280" y2="978" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>
        <line x1="495" y1="978" x2="520" y2="978" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>
        <line x1="735" y1="978" x2="760" y2="978" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#abig)"/>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-brand-muted">
        One diagram, two views. On the left, a real die photograph of the Broadcom BCM7019 Arm SoC; on the right, the Arm
        Trusted Firmware-A cold boot chain. Each <span class="font-semibold text-brand-text">white numbered box (1 to 6)</span> is
        a boot stage; each <span class="font-semibold text-brand-text">coloured card</span> is a piece of hardware or memory,
        and its colour follows from its spot on the die (or an off-die board chip) through to the stage it serves. BL1 uses the
        clock, watchdog, boot ROM, OTP fuses and crypto engine; BL2 loads the later images from
        <span style="color:#854f0b"> Flash</span> into <span style="color:#0f6e56">Trusted SRAM</span>; a failed signature check
        halts the SoC; and once the verified chain crosses into the normal world, BL33 boots the OS in
        <span style="color:#185fa5"> DRAM</span>. Block positions on the die are approximate: the BCM7019 is not officially
        annotated, so the markers show where such structures typically sit. Source: {" "}
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Broadcom_BCM7019_STB_SoC_die_shot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:text-accent-emerald">Broadcom BCM7019 STB SoC die shot</a>{" "}
        by Martijn Boer (Flickr), public domain.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <!-- Step by step -->
    <h3 class="mb-4 mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-brand-text">Step by step</h3>
    <ol class="space-y-4">
      {steps.map((s) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-0.5 inline-flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-white" style="background:#534AB7">{s.n}</span>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-brand-body"><strong class="text-brand-text">{s.title}.</strong> {s.body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <!-- Two connections -->
    <h3 class="mb-3 mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-brand-text">Two connections worth calling out</h3>
    <ul class="ml-5 list-disc space-y-3 marker:text-accent-green">
      {connections.map((c) => (
        <li class="leading-relaxed text-brand-body"><strong class="text-brand-text">{c.title}</strong> {c.body}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-brand-border bg-brand-bg px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-brand-muted">
      Note: this is one representative TF-A cold boot on AArch64; details vary by platform. BL32 is optional, and on
      AArch32 the five stages collapse to four (there is no BL31, and BL32 such as SP_MIN plays the EL3 runtime role).
      Exactly when the clock, watchdog, crypto engine and TrustZone controller are programmed is platform-specific;
      they are shown at one plausible point in the BL1 / BL2 flow, not as a fixed hardware sequence.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

